`suggestion`: A keyboard user who presses "Move up" twice on a variant ends up back where they started. The variant rows are keyed by their index (`frontend/src/scenes/feature-flags/RulesV2ExperimentFields.tsx:133`), so after a move React keeps the same button element in the same row position. Focus stays on that position, which now holds the neighbouring variant. The second press moves the neighbour back.

The rule cards in this editor avoid this on purpose. `ruleKeys` keeps one key per rule (`featureFlagRulesV2EditorLogic.ts:119`), and `FeatureFlagRulesV2Editor.test.tsx:128` checks that focus follows a moved rule. The comment at `RulesV2ExperimentFields.tsx:132` covers row state but not focus. A row key held in local state gives the variant rows the same behaviour:

```tsx
let lastVariantRowKey = 0
const nextVariantRowKey = (): number => ++lastVariantRowKey

// in RulesV2VariantsField
const [rowKeys, setRowKeys] = useState(() => variants.map(nextVariantRowKey))
const moveVariant = (from: number, to: number): void => {
    setVariants(moved(variants, from, to))
    setRowKeys(moved(rowKeys, from, to))
}
const removeVariant = (at: number): void => {
    setVariants(variants.filter((_, i) => i !== at))
    setRowKeys(rowKeys.filter((_, i) => i !== at))
}
const addVariant = (): void => {
    setVariants([...variants, newVariant(returnType, variants.length)])
    setRowKeys([...rowKeys, nextVariantRowKey()])
}
// on the row: key={rowKeys[variantIndex] ?? `row-${variantIndex}`}
```

A copy of the rule-card focus test that clicks `rules-v2-move-variant-down` would fail before this change and pass after it.

cases.before:
`suggestion`: Keyboard focus does not follow a moved variant. The variant rows use `key={variantIndex}`, so after a move React keeps each row's DOM elements at the same index. Pressing "Move up" twice on C in [A, B, C] first gives [A, C, B]. Focus stays on the third row's button, which now renders B, so the second press moves B back and the order returns to [A, B, C].

Consider keeping stable row keys in `useState` and updating them in the add, move and remove handlers, as `ruleKeys` does for rule cards. These keys would replace `key={variantIndex}` and its "Rows hold no state of their own" comment:

```tsx
let lastVariantRowKey = 0
const nextVariantRowKey = (): number => ++lastVariantRowKey

const [rowKeys, setRowKeys] = useState(() => variants.map(nextVariantRowKey))
// move: setVariants(moved(variants, from, to)); setRowKeys(moved(rowKeys, from, to))
// remove: filter both arrays by index
// add: append nextVariantRowKey()
// row: key={rowKeys[variantIndex] ?? `row-${variantIndex}`}
```

A focus test like the one at [`FeatureFlagRulesV2Editor.test.tsx:128`](https://github.com/PostHog/posthog/blob/35cd9393503528fb30813e3b5de37a619100c7ad/frontend/src/scenes/feature-flags/FeatureFlagRulesV2Editor.test.tsx#L128), but moving a variant instead of a rule, would check that focus follows the variant.

